card c21
kind: item
type: item
item: u1-x-unstable-which

card c05
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Unstable nuclei can change
figure: decay-figure
figure_at: 2

body:
    An unstable nucleus can change into a different nucleus. When it does, it gives off [[radiation|radiation]]. This is called [[radioactivity|radioactivity]], and an atom that does it is radioactive. It happens because the nucleus is unstable, not because anyone makes it happen.

card c09
kind: item
type: item
item: u1-l5-choice-unstable-does
connected: true

card c06
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Radioactivity is natural
figure: natural-sources
figure_at: 2

body:
    Nobody had to invent radioactivity. Unstable atoms are in rocks, in the air and in food. A banana and your own body hold a little natural potassium-40. People can make radioactive atoms too, but most of the radioactivity around us is natural.

card c07
kind: item
type: item
item: u1-l5-tom-human-made
connected: true

card c08
kind: item
type: item
item: u1-l5-tom-body-stable

card c22
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: What comes next

body:
    So now you know that some nuclei can change all by themselves. In the next unit you will find out what comes out when one does, and how fast it happens.

card c11
kind: teach
type: explain
tier: deeper
scored: 0
title: Go deeper: what holds a nucleus together
figure: force-balance
figure_at: 1

body:
    Protons are all positive, so they push each other apart. A very strong force inside the nucleus, the [[strong-force|strong force]], helps hold it together. Some nuclei hold together well. Others do not.
